is  series for so long is that when you  actually think about it  each arc of this series has a great  premise  i'm a big fan of science fiction and  i've read a lot of classic  and modern sci-fi and i've also watched  a ton of science fiction movies  tv shows and anime and believe it or not  the initial premise of each sao arc has  echoes of some of the most influential  and critically acclaimed works of  science fiction  let's start with the very first arc in  the story the aincrad arc  the premise of this first arc is that a  bunch of everyday people become  trapped inside a new virtual reality  game they are unable to lock out and  leave the virtual world and if they die  in the game they die in real life when i  first began watching sao  i thought that this was an extremely  interesting premise and i absolutely  loved the first  episode of the anime i thought it did a  great job of setting up the world and  the ending of the first episode  compelled me to keep watching to see  what happens next  this idea of being trapped in a virtual  reality based game is certainly nothing  new  and classic sci-fi franchises like tron  already explored the same territory  decades ago  not to mention that other anime  franchises like dot hack  did it too but i actually thought that  sao did a good job of modernizing this  trapped in a virtual reality setting  in the post-world of warcraft era a lot  has been said about kirito the  protagonist of seo being  too overpowered but i was willing to  look past it in the first  arc because the series cleverly  established that kirito was one of the  1000 beta testers for the original sao  game  so it made sense that since he had more  experience playing it kirito would have  an advantage over most other players in  sao i'll save my issues with sao  including the aincrad arc for a little  bit later but now let's talk about the  second  arc in the series the alpheim arc the  premise of this arc is that  after most of the surviving players of  the original swordart online game were  able to log out successfully and wake up  some survivors of sao including asuna  failed to wake up for some  unknown reason we later learned that  asuna's consciousness is being held  captive inside alfheim online the game  that is the successor to  sao and this is all part of a plan by  sugo nobuyuki who was able to hack  into the sao server and reroute the  minds of the missing  sao players so that he can use them as  test subjects in his research on how to  manipulate  memories nobuyuki also intended to  inherit  asuna's family fortune by persuading her  father to let him marry asuna while she  is still  unconscious now i have no idea how this  would even work  but maybe japan has marriage laws that  are more inclusive of the consciousness  challenged anyway this idea of hacking  people's brains and manipulating their  minds is another  extremely cool sci-fi concept with a lot  of potential  especially as modern technology  continues to move towards virtual  reality and cybernetic implants and  enhancements  just think of ghost in the shell and how  heavily the idea of hacking a cybernetic  brain  plays into the story as a whole now am i  saying that sao does as good of a job in  handling these intriguing sci-fi  concepts as  ghost in the shell does no definitely  not and i will explain  why but now let's look at the third arc  the phantom  bullet arc the premise is as follows  within a shooter game called  gun gale online those players who are  killed by a specific individual known as  death gun somehow end up dying in real  life  those who are shot in the game by death  gun instantly disconnect from the game  and turn up dead in real life a few days  later here again we have a situation  where the setup to the ark  is incredibly interesting we have a  truly fascinating mystery in which the  line between the fantasy world of the  game and the real world is blurred  and we are desperately trying to figure  out how it is possible for someone to  kill a player in a game  and also have that person instantly die  in real life  this is not the same situation as with  the aincrad arc where all the players  were trapped and everyone who died in  the game died in real life  instead only this one mysterious villain  called  death gun has the ability to kill  someone in the game and have them  also die in real life and i remember  that i was personally extremely  interested in figuring out how this was  even possible  i also felt that this premise explored  some very intriguing questions about the  modern world and just how connected our  online lives  are to our physical lives i mean we all  spend so much time on the internet  interacting with all sorts of strangers  so much so that we sometimes forget that  meeting the wrong person online  can be pretty dangerous and death gun is  the living embodiment  of that very thing again this is another  premise that i loved a premise that kept  me hooked and that made me think about  the philosophical implications of our  increasingly high-tech society which is  the effect that all good science fiction  tends to have on me  then we have mother's rosario which is  technically a side story rather than an  independent arc  the premise here is that people who are  physically disabled or terminally ill in  the physical world can now  thanks to modern technology live a  fulfilling life in the virtual world  where they are not hindered by the  physical limitations of their bodies  yet again here we have a brilliant  sci-fi  concept and this same concept has been  explored in a number of highly praised  works of science fiction  including of course the critically  acclaimed sci-fi series  black mirror one of its episodes  entitled san junipero has basically the  same  exact premise and with technology  advancing as rapidly as it is  it isn't too far-fetched to think that  in a few years people with physical  limitations could abandon the physical  world and embrace  virtual reality instead but then the  question is  where do we draw the line how long  before individuals who might be  perfectly healthy physically decide  that they prefer the virtual world to  their real lives  some would say that a troubling  percentage of people is already heading  in this direction so how long before our  society has to address the serious  consequences that will emerge as a  result  of this shift from the physical to the  virtual these are all  important and profoundly interesting  questions that  if handled well can make for some  amazing science fiction  and finally let's look at the premise  for the sao movie  called ordinal scale in ordinal scale  the setting moves from a virtual world  into augmented reality and the line  between the real world and the virtual  world becomes even more blurry as real  physical spaces are transformed into  fantastical settings through the power  of augmented reality or  ar ar technology has been growing  rapidly in the past  five to ten years and the phenomenon of  pokemon go  back in 2016 demonstrated that the  premise of ordinal scale is both  plausible  and highly interesting now of course the  initial  avalanche of interest that pokemon go  generated when it first came out didn't  last  and realistically that level of hype and  popularity for  any game is unsustainable back when it  first came out the streets were  literally full of people playing pokemon  go  children were playing together with  their parents and grandparents it was  truly a phenomenon  but even though the popularity of  pokemon go is nowhere near those initial  levels  there are still a lot of people playing  pokemon go even now so this game was by  no means a flop  and it isn't hard to imagine that as  augmented reality technology advances  something like the ar mmorpg that we see  in the ordinal scale movie  could appear in our own world in the  near future  so there we are again we have a  fascinating and plausible sci-fi concept  that has the potential to create some  amazing stories  so then why is it that despite the fact  that each story arc has an intriguing  and promising premise  sword art online continues to receive so  much hate even though i feel that the  show's faults have been greatly  exaggerated over the years  i think i know why sao has failed to  live up to its full potential  thus far while i love the science  fiction elements in sao and the initial  premise of each arc  i also kind of hate the fact that the  character development in sao  fails to live up to the promising  premise in order to create a great story  you need a great  premise but in order to make the story  truly amazing you also need great  characters who go through meaningful  development throughout the course of the  series  granted sao spends a lot of time on the  character of kirito and on his  relationship with asuna which i like  and i was happy to see their  relationship actually progress in a real  way  something that doesn't often happen in  harem anime however the real  issue with sao is the lack of character  development for  everyone else other than kirito and  asuna and this includes the villains  you can't fully explore a cool sci-fi  premise if you don't  develop your characters because the  social economic and philosophical impact  of modern technology can only be fully  explored through its effects on the  characters within the story  think for a moment about the side  characters in sao  including lisbeth the blacksmith lifa  the cousin  cinone silica klein and so on  all of these characters play an  important role at some point in the  story but then  after their main story arc finishes they  simply fade into the background and  they're just  kind of there they're just hanging  around and not doing anything too  important while we focus on kirito  asuna and whichever new characters are  relevant in the current arc  compare this to another shonen series  the seven deadly sins or nanatsuno  taizai  sure that series could have just focused  on the relationship between the captain  of the sins meliodus  and his love interest elizabeth in the  same way that sao focuses on kirito and  asuna but instead the seventh deadly sin  spends  a lot of time developing the other  characters including  the six remaining members of the sins  each character has their own unique  backstory and the relationships between  the characters continue to grow and  develop as the series goes on  furthermore these characters do not  simply fade into the background  once a new arc begins instead each  member of the seven deadly sins has  their own  important role to play in the story even  now as the manga  approaches chapter 300 yui the ai  is also a great example of sao's failure  to develop its characters  the idea of an advanced computer program  becoming self-aware and developing  artificial intelligence is another major  theme in science fiction  and it has been explored in everything  from william gibson's neuromancer and  the tron  franchise to ghost in the shell and the  terminator to name just a few  when yui was first introduced i loved  this idea of having an  ai become self-aware and begin to  interact with actual people within the  sao  game but after she is initially  introduced yui  also becomes a relatively static side  character who considers kirito and asuna  to be her parents even though they're  not  since yui possesses superior artificial  intelligence  a lot more focus could have been placed  on her development  how her understanding of human beings  and their relationships changes and  matures  and a lot more time could have been  spent on what the existence of such an  advanced ai means for the society in  which sao is taking place  the fact that such an advanced ai exists  an ai that is able to interact  directly with human beings is a  revolutionary technological development  and yet yui is relegated to a side  character whose primary role seems to be  to act  cute and help kirito and asuna once in a  while with technical support  and don't even get me started on the  villains  kaiaba the creator of the original sao  and nerve gear seems to be interesting  at first  but as we learn more about him we  realize that his character is shallow  and his motivations for doing what he  did  make no sense i mean sure we can  understand why kaibab would want to have  full control of the virtual world he  created  power is definitely a strong motivator  for human beings  but why did kayaba decide that thousands  of innocent people needed to die  in sao he could have still controlled  the virtual world without having anyone  actually die and even if he wanted to  keep people trapped in the game he could  have held their consciousnesses captive  just like nobuyuki aka fairy king oberon  does to asuna and the others  literally no one had to die let alone  innocent teenagers who just wanted to  play a game  kaiba eventually scans his brain and  permanently transfers his consciousness  into the virtual world which is another  thought-provoking sci-fi  concept but the full potential of this  premise  is never explored and not much is done  with kaioba's character  even though there were so many  possibilities to do more  our boy fairy king oberon is also a very  one-dimensional villain he is an evil  psycho who wants money and power  but other than being extremely punchable  for the evil douchebag that he  is there is nothing relatable or complex  about his character  what drove him to become the way that he  is what is his belief system and the  true driving force behind his actions  human beings are complicated it is very  rare for someone to just be purely  evil or purely good but there is nothing  complicated about  oberon he's just an abusive psycho who  wants money and power  other villains like death gun also turn  out to be one-dimensional and their  motivations turn out to be pretty  dubious once we learn  more 